# CHEESE indexed database catalog — the databases available for on-prem download.
#
# One line per database:
#
#     <folder>|<bytes>|<objects>
#
# `folder` is the folder name in the delivery bucket, and the directory name on
# disk once downloaded. `bytes` and `objects` are informational: `cheese
# configure-dbs` shows the size so you can check free disk space before starting,
# and `cheese download-dbs` totals it for its space preflight. A blank size means
# "not measured" — the download still works, the estimate is just incomplete.
#
# Why a shipped file instead of a bucket listing: the read-only role your access
# key assumes can read the database folders but cannot list the bucket root, so
# there is nothing for the CLI to enumerate. `cheese configure-dbs` verifies every
# entry against the bucket and marks anything outside your entitlement as
# "NOT AVAILABLE", so a line you cannot pull shows up as such rather than failing
# hours into a download.
#
# Compose: you do not edit this file. Run `cheese configure-dbs`, set the
# databases you want to `yes`, then `cheese download-dbs --dest <folder>`.
#
# Kubernetes: take the folder names from here. Each database you want becomes an
# entry under `database.databases` in your values file, keyed by the database's
# CANONICAL name, with `output_directory` set to the folder below — the
# DB_CANONICAL_NAME table in scripts/_engine-config maps folder -> canonical.
# `dataSync` then fetches exactly the entries you enabled.

